Yes — this application is very similar to the P2P Edge RetryConfig, so you can apply the same Reactor-based approach here. I would make the change directly in this RetryConfig rather than creating another listener class.

Recommended updated RetryConfig

package com.cardinalhealth.advship.tracking.resource.config;

import com.cardinalhealth.ofl.next.logging.library.LogBuilder;
import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClientResponseException;
import reactor.util.retry.Retry;
import reactor.util.retry.Retry.RetrySignal;

import java.time.Duration;

@Slf4j
@Configuration
public class RetryConfig {

    /**
     * Creates a Retry object with a maximum number of attempts and an initial delay.
     *
     * @param maxAttempts the maximum number of retry attempts
     * @param initialDelaySeconds the initial delay in seconds before the first retry
     * @return a Retry object configured with the specified parameters
     */
    public Retry createRetry(int maxAttempts, long initialDelaySeconds) {
        return Retry.backoff(
                    maxAttempts,
                    Duration.ofSeconds(initialDelaySeconds)
                )
                .maxBackoff(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
                .jitter(0.5)
                .filter(this::isRetryable)

                // Log every retry attempt
                .doBeforeRetry(this::logRetry)

                .onRetryExhaustedThrow((retryBackoffSpec, retrySignal) -> {
                    logRetryExhausted(retrySignal);

                    Throwable lastError = retrySignal.failure();

                    if (lastError instanceof RuntimeException) {
                        return lastError;
                    }

                    return new RuntimeException(
                            "Retries exhausted for the request",
                            lastError
                    );
                });
    }

    /**
     * Determines if a request is retryable based on the exception type and status code.
     * Retry will happen for 5XX errors and also for network/unknown exceptions.
     *
     * @param throwable the exception thrown during the request
     * @return true if the request is retryable, false otherwise
     */
    public boolean isRetryable(Throwable throwable) {

        if (throwable instanceof WebClientResponseException ex) {
            int statusCode = ex.getStatusCode().value();

            return statusCode >= 500 && statusCode < 600;
        }

        return true;
    }

    /**
     * Logs exception details for every retry attempt.
     */
    private void logRetry(RetrySignal retrySignal) {

        Throwable failure = retrySignal.failure();

        LogBuilder logBuilder = new LogBuilder()
                .withMessage("Retrying external API request")
                .withField(
                        "retryAttempt",
                        retrySignal.totalRetries() + 1
                )
                .withField(
                        "exceptionType",
                        failure.getClass().getSimpleName()
                )
                .withField(
                        "exceptionMessage",
                        failure.getMessage()
                );

        if (failure instanceof WebClientResponseException ex) {
            logBuilder.withField(
                    "statusCode",
                    ex.getStatusCode().value()
            );
        }

        log.error(logBuilder.toString(), failure);
    }

    /**
     * Logs the final exception when all retry attempts are exhausted.
     */
    private void logRetryExhausted(RetrySignal retrySignal) {

        Throwable failure = retrySignal.failure();

        LogBuilder logBuilder = new LogBuilder()
                .withMessage("Retries exhausted for the request")
                .withField(
                        "totalRetries",
                        retrySignal.totalRetries()
                )
                .withField(
                        "exceptionType",
                        failure.getClass().getSimpleName()
                )
                .withField(
                        "exceptionMessage",
                        failure.getMessage()
                );

        if (failure instanceof WebClientResponseException ex) {
            logBuilder.withField(
                    "statusCode",
                    ex.getStatusCode().value()
            );
        }

        log.error(logBuilder.toString(), failure);
    }
}

What changed?

The main addition is:

.doBeforeRetry(this::logRetry)

This acts as your Reactor equivalent of a retry listener.

So the flow is:

API call
   ↓
Exception
   ↓
isRetryable()
   ↓
true
   ↓
doBeforeRetry()
   ↓
Log exception + retry attempt
   ↓
Backoff
   ↓
Retry

For example:

Retrying external API request
retryAttempt=1
exceptionType=WebClientResponseException
exceptionMessage=503 Service Unavailable
statusCode=503

Then if it fails again:

Retrying external API request
retryAttempt=2
...

And after the final failure:

Retries exhausted for the request
totalRetries=3
exceptionType=...
exceptionMessage=...

Why remove the current log from isRetryable()?

Currently you have:

log.error(new LogBuilder()
        .withMessage("Is this request retryable?")
        .withField("statusCode", statusCode)
        .toString());

I'd remove it because it doesn't tell you much and can log even when the request won't actually be retried.

Instead:

isRetryable()
    → only decides TRUE/FALSE

doBeforeRetry()
    → logs only when a retry is actually happening

That's a cleaner separation and is consistent with the approach you already implemented in P2P Edge.

One thing to check before changing this: find where createRetry() is being used in this application. If multiple WebClient classes use this same RetryConfig, this change will give them the retry logging centrally, which is exactly what you want for this card.